  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/bc/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_48.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "When a steel supplier named Mr. Carter suddenly backs out of a deal with Tony Stark, he drives over to investigate, only to discover that Carter is being held captive by a \"Mister Doll,\" who is coercing him to sign over his company to his captor.\n\nStark attacks as Iron Man, but the mysterious Mister Doll quickly changes the shape of the doll he's holding to look like Iron Man and begins to crush him, making Tony feel it inside his armor.&nbsp; He has no choice but to retreat and actually falls off the balcony into water below.&nbsp; Thinking that his enemy is defeated, Mr. Doll refashions the head of the doll to take the form of Carter once again and continues to threaten the terrified man.\n\nBut the change in appearance of the doll has also released his control over Iron Man, who drags himself out of the water.&nbsp; Needing a charge to keep his heart pumping, he barely makes it back to his office in time to plug in.\n\nTony realizes that he needs recharging too often and blames the ponderous golden suit, which is too heavy and saps too much power.&nbsp; He'll have to design a lighter version so that it won't be such a strain on his heart.&nbsp; He soon designs a sleeker, red and gold version and demonstrates its new abilities, including the fact that, being lighter, he can pack more gizmos into it!&nbsp; And his headgear can now show more expression.\n\nMeanwhile, the local police precinct calls in Mr. Carter to urge him to file a complaint.&nbsp; There simply <i>has</i> to be a reason that he is the third millionaire Mr. Doll has forced into signing over his fortune.&nbsp; The police are <i>baffled</i> as to why this is happening and vow to figure out who is <i>next</i> on Mr. Doll's list.\n\nAs the fourth richest man in the city, it turns out that Tony Stark is Mr. Doll's next target!&nbsp; The police recognize the pattern and go to warn Stark, who agrees to be a decoy so they can catch Mr. Doll's in the act.\n\nPretending to be interested in Pepper to \"ask her for a date,\" and asking to be alone with her, the surprised woman soon finds that Tony did this only as a ruse to shake the police guard.&nbsp; Tony quickly slips away through a secret exit on the pretext that he's off to find Iron Man, leaving Pepper alone to sob in disappointment\n\nSure enough, Mr. Doll soon appears, and the new Iron Man stands, ready to face him.&nbsp; But Doll is ready, alters the doll's features to resemble Iron Man, and begins to twist his doll's arm, which soon causes Stark intense pain.&nbsp; Yet, because the new armor is lighter, the strain on his heart is therefore lessened, and he's able to withstand the pain better.&nbsp; He <i>pretends</i> to succumb to Doll's will so that he'll think he now controls Iron Man.\n\nMr. Doll reveals that the doll came from a witch doctor in Africa, and, believing that Iron Man has been frightened enough in the face of his awesome power, he refashions it into Tony Stark's image in order to force the weapons manufacturer to come to <i>him</i>.&nbsp; This causes Iron Man to turn away in agony, though not willing to let it show for fear of his dual identity being exposed.\n\nPretending to flee in fear, Iron Man leaves the scene and locks himself in a lab to prevent himself from returning, and even as the pain increases, Tony Stark puts his genius to work and manages to fashion the only counter weapon he feels will be effective.\n\nWhen he next confronts Mr. Doll as Iron Man, he wields the weapon, which is a transistor-powered gadget that literally refashions the voodoo doll while still in the hands of its operator.&nbsp; Iron Man quickly reshapes it into the form of Mr. Doll himself, which surprises the villain enough for him to drop it.&nbsp; And in seconds, he too crumbles to the ground, totally defeated!\n\nAfter Tony reappears, he and Happy realize that Pepper is still in the sealed room all by herself, and she is so incensed that she storms off as they watch on in bafflement.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A story that limps along until the new armor arrives, providing the only real spark.\nYou read it for the upgrade, but the plot crumbles as quickly as the villain does.",
  "subtitle": "Red and Gold Revamp -- And Little Else",
  "review_text": "Mister Doll seems like a cross between the Puppet Master and a bokor (sometimes spelled bocor), a sort of sorcerer in Haitian Vodou.&nbsp; In fact, the voodoo vibe becomes <i>unmistakable</i> once he refashions the doll into the shape of Iron Man's helmet.\n[ASSET: center | MisterDollsPower | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: left | ItsTheIronSuit | width=11%] Though Stark slips away from his antagonist, the encounter opens his eyes, revealing that his ponderous iron suit is to blame for so many of the power shortages he experiences on a regular basis.&nbsp; The constant need to recharge it has brought him close to the brink of death on multiple occasions.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ArmorFinished | width=17%] [ASSET: right | TheNewIronMan | width=31%] There <i>has</i> to be a way to redesign his armor to make it lighter in weight without sacrificing strength.\n[BR]\n[BR] After working in his lab for several hours, pages 8 through 10 gradually unveil a new, sleeker, lighter-weight armor -- the clear highlight of the story -- with artist Ditko showing step-by-step how the suit can be assembled.&nbsp; Furthermore, being more efficient allows it to hold more weaponry.\n[BR]\n[BR] The new-look Iron Man stands fully-revealed at the end of page 10, ready to go into action!\n\nWhile the new armor is interesting, page 12 shows that Tony isn't above manipulation, intentionally playing with Pepper's emotions, all with the motive of shaking his police guard so that he can change to Iron Man under their noses and be ready for Mr. Doll when he eventually arrives.\n\n[ASSET: right | PepperThrowsHerselfAtStark | width=14%] Happy and Pepper already had a one-panel bickering session about every other page, which is apparently supposed to be the \"charm\" of the book.&nbsp; But I found it growing tiresome.\n[BR]\n[BR] And by far the low point of the book was the moment when Pepper throws herself at her boss, which reduces her to little more than a romantic foil with hardly any personality beyond [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Oh, my employer <i>loves </i>me!&nbsp; Life is <i>wonderful</i>!\"] It reduces her entire emotional arc to a single, shallow beat.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And then she learns it was all for show on the part of Stark)]\n\nI'm sorry, but [SPAN: severity=error; text=this isn't character development.&nbsp; This is nothing less than forcing a supporting character to punch in for the month], and <i>honestly</i>, this book needs a love triangle about as much as Iron Man needs a second chestplate!\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe last six pages of the book were perfunctory, and it seemed that neither Stan nor Steve were really interested in the disposition of Mr. Doll, other than to find a quick way to [SPAN: severity=error; text=get rid of him] now that Ditko's new Iron Man armor had debuted.\n\n[ASSET: left | IveWon | width=16%] I yawned along with everyone else after Iron Man easily reshapes the voodoo doll into the image of Mr. Doll himself, who <i>naturally </i>drops it in surprise.&nbsp; And without any more fanfare than that, the villain crumbles to the ground, and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved!]&nbsp; It's a resolution so abrupt that it barely qualifies as a climax.\n[BR]\n[BR] Note how thin, scrawny, and [SPAN: severity=warning; text=downright <b>flat</b>] the figure of Iron Man is in that image.&nbsp; It's a perfect metaphor for how flat this plot was as a whole.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nObviously the main takeaway from this issue was the newly-designed Iron Man armor (which was even touted on the cover), and, say what you will about the <i>rest</i> of the story and the weakness of its villain-of-the-month, [SPAN: severity=success; text=it is nice to see Iron Man in an outfit that doesn't look like a ponderous suit of armor you'd find in a knight museum.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=If this was Mr. Ditko's doing (and the evidence seems to suggest this), then let's give the man the credit he deserves.]\n\nBut design features aside, I found his rendition of the armor in general (both pre- and post-new look) to have a more painted-on look to it.&nbsp; At times, it didn't look like armor at all, but more like Stark wearing gold (or red and gold) <b>pajamas</b>.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=You call that <i>armor</i>, Steve?&nbsp; I call it a <i>Romper Room</i> jumpsuit!]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=And <i>boy</i> did that one image at the end remind me of the cover of DC's classic <i>Flash</i> #126 (which featured a flattened Flash).]\n\nDitko's ability to design suits may be impressive, but his ability to illustrate them in action doesn't seem... <i>well</i>... \"suited\" to carrying this feature over the long term, and I sure hope we get Don Heck back soon (who I think does a far better job).\n\nAnd let's not forget this month's <i>villain</i>... [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]\n\nMr. Doll was silly, unimaginative, and ill-conceived, and as I mentioned above, was little more than a cross between the Puppet Master and a witch-doctor.&nbsp; Please, Marvel, whatever you do, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=leave him in the garbage bin of history!]\n\nThe updated armor was good, and saved the story for the most part, and I even applaud Tony for realizing the design flaw he had on his hands.&nbsp; That's what a good inventor does:&nbsp; keep tinkering with the design until you get an actually functional model, and so, in that regard, [SPAN: severity=success; text=the issue has a genuine, and seemingly-lasting, highlight.]\n\nBut the one-off (I hope) villain threatened to crumble all that goodwill every bit as much as the villain himself crumpled to the ground harmlessly in the end, as the final six pages of the plot simply went through the motions.\n\nThe final page, showing the scorned Pepper Potts storming away in anger, only mirrored my own disappointment as to how little there was to offer this month.&nbsp; It's a deflating finish to a story that [SPAN: severity=success; text=only came alive when the new armor appeared], and without it, the story wouldn't have been readable at all.\n\n[DEVNOTE: Mr. Doll later becomes Brother Grimm, or Brothers Grimm, as seen in Spider-Woman in the 1970s]",
  "ownership": "I got a reader-grade copy as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $23, which sold on eBay in Jan 2021 for $169, netting me over $122.",
  "highlight": "Iron Man faces Mr. Doll in a flat tale saved only by the debut of his new red‑and‑gold armor."
}